Zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) is a type of Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which shows promising application in the field of bacterial infection, owing to its excellent biocompatibility. Here, we report the encapsulation of silver nanoparticles $(\mathsf{A g\ N P s})$ in ZIF-8, accompanied with embedding of physcion (Phy) to obtain Ag-Phy@ZIF-8 with efficient and intelligent synergistic antimicrobial capabilities. Due to the micro-acidic environment around the bacteria, the release of silver and Phy shows a controlled released. Further, the AgPhy@ZIF-8 is modified by hyaluronate (HA), denoted as Ag-Phy $@$ ZIF-8@HA, which has a strong inhibitory effect on the growth of both E. coli $(99.1\%)$ and S. aureus $(99.5\%)$ , with no impacting on cell growth, showing good biocompatibility. Thus, these pH-responsive biocomposites have the potential application on smart wound excipients for bacterial infections.
## 1. Introduction
Bacterial infections are a serious medical problem [1], being treated with the administration of antibiotic agents. However, with the misuse of antibiotics, bacteria is becoming increasingly resistant to them [2], which certainly increases the risk of infection. Bacteria, such as E. coli, S. aureus, P. aeruginosa, have already been shown to possess antibiotic resistance [3]. While most of the antibiotics are toxic and harmful [4], which give rise to the realization that drug resistance in bacteria is causing serious harm to human beings [5,6]. These bacteria are spread through air-borne transmission, contact transmission, and contaminated surfaces. They also spread rapidly in the wound, causing it fail to heal [7].
Hyaluronic acid (HA) is a natural polysaccharide and an important component of the extracellular matrix. It possesses good hydrophilic and biodegradable properties [8]. Due to the unique properties of HA, it is commonly used for drug delivery [9], immunomodulation [10] and as a bacteriostatic agent [11]. Moreover, it is well known that pathogenic bacteria are able to secrete HA lyases that are capable to degrade native HA, which is a known marker for virulence. In this context, nanocarriers modified with HA have been shown to enhance bacterial-targeted drug release and to reduce many side effects from antibiotic drugs [12]. However, HA is very easily hydrolyzed, which is usually modified to hyaluronate in practical applications.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), three-dimensional porous mate­ rials [13], have the advantages of high porosity, large specific surface area, good biocompatibility, high stability and adjustable structure [14], which can be widely used in gas storage and separation [15], catalysis [16], drug delivery [17] and antibacterial applications [18]. ZIF-8 is one of the most biocompatible MOFs and it is widely used in biomedical applications due to its unique pore size structure that facilitates drug encapsulation and controlled release of drugs [19]. It has been reported previously that the metal ion $(Z\mathrm{n}^{2+})$ of ZIF-8 [20] is a beneficial element for humans and the ligand dimethylimidazole of it is also good bacterial inhibitor [21]. It has been reported that pH-responsive ZIF-8 can be metabolized in living organisms [22]. Moreover, ZIF-8 can be dis­ integrated instantly under strong acid conditions, while degraded slowly in a slightly acidic environment. Due to the wound surroundings are in a slightly acidic environment, so ZIF-8 is an excellent choice as sustained release drugs carrier and applying in wound excipients.
The natural drug Physcion (Phy) is an anthraquinone compound mainly found in the Chinese herbal medicines, such as Polygoni Multi­ flori Radix [23], Polygonum cuspidatum [24], and lichen [25], which has a wide range of pharmacological effects, including antibacterial and anti-inflammatory [26], hepatoprotective [27] and anti-cancer [28] etc. Many synthetic antibacterial agents have been developed, and their production is costly, time-consuming, unsafe and many side effects [29]. Therefore, it is a wise choice to directly conduct research in drugs found in nature and exploit them as novel antibacterial agents [30]. In other words, it is necessary to develop a novel and effective method of loading Phy into nanomaterials, due to its easy agglomeration in water and poor bioavailability [31].
Silver nanoparticles (Ag NPs) have unique physicochemical proper­ ties that make them promising for antibacterial [32], electronic [33], and detection analysis applications [34]. In particular, Ag NPs have a wide range of antibacterial applications, due to their large specific surface area, high surface activity, and strong adsorption capacity [35,36,37]. However, silver nanoparticles, in solution and in physio­ logical conditions, can agglomerate into clusters [38,39]. This, in return, results in inconsistent antibacterial activity and pharmacokinetics [40]. Thus, the development of a new material that can maintain the anti­ bacterial activity of Ag NPs as well as improve their controlled release is our main goal [41,42].
In this work, we developed a novel composite material based on MOF effectively encapsulating Ag NPs and embedding Phy. We also used the biocompatible and non-toxic HA to modify on the surface and strengthen the antibacterial effect, as shown in Scheme 1. The new multifunctional material obtained is noted as $\mathrm{Ag-Phy}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8@\mathrm{HA},$ which aims to overcome bacterial drug resistance, improve safety and solve the problem of uncontrolled release and accumulation of silver. We found it has a synergistic antibacterial effect and an extremely strong inhibitory effect on common bacteria.
## 2. Results and discussion
## 2.1. Preparation and characterization of Ag-Phy@ZIF-8@HA NPs
Phy $@$ ZIF-8 was synthesized by the “one-pot method” through selfassembly encapsulating Phy in ZIF-8, while Ag-Phy@ZIF-8 was syn­ thesized by the novel method of “two-solvent method” to load Ag NPs inside the MOF pores. Fig. 1a and b shows the SEM image of AgPhy@ZIF-8, which morphology exhibits a dodecahedral crystal struc­ ture similar to the image of ZIF-8 in Fig. S1. The TEM image in Fig. 1c confirms the successful encapsulation of Ag NPs inside ZIF-8, subsequently, the elements in Ag-Phy $@$ ZIF-8 were analyzed by EDS, and it can be concluded from Fig. S2 that Ag and Zn elements are present in the ZIF-8. It is also clear from the elemental mapping analysis diagram in Fig. 1d that Ag, Zn, N and O elements are uniformly distributed in ZIF-8. From the UV–Vis spectrogram Fig. S3, it can be observed that Phy has the highest absorption peak at $433\mathrm{nm}$ , and Phy $@$ ZIF-8 has a slight red shift at the corresponding position, indicating Phy was successfully encapsulated in ZIF-8. And ${\bf A g}@\mathbf{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ has an absorption peak around $415\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which is mainly the absorption peak of Ag NPs [43].
The structural characteristics of the crystals were characterized by XRD and FT-IR. In Fig. 2a, ZIF-8 with a square sodiumite topology shows characteristic peaks at $2\theta=7.40^{\circ}$ , $10.45^{\circ}$ , $12.78^{\circ}$ and $14.71^{\circ}$ , which is consistent with previous reports in the literature [44]. In contrast, the characteristic peaks of ${\bf A g}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ , Phy $@$ ZIF-8, and Ag-Phy@ZIF-8 were similar to those of ZIF-8, indicating that the successful encapsulation of Phy and $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ did not change the structural features of the crystals. From the fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra Fig. 2b, it can be seen that the characteristic peak at $3414~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the FT-IR spectrum of Phy is caused by the vibration of the phenolic hydroxyl group, and an addi­ tional peak at about $3200~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ exist in the Phy@ZIF-8 and Ag$\mathrm{Phy}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ , except for the characteristic peaks of ZIF-8 [45,46], which indicates that the phenolic hydroxyl group of Phy red shift and confirms that Phy is excellently encapsulated in ZIF-8. These characteristic peaks are consistent with those previously reported [47].
The nitrogen adsorption and desorption curves of ZIF-8 and AgPhy@ZIF-8 in Fig. 2c shows that they are both typical type I curves, which indicated the ZIF-8 wrapped Ag and Phy still retain their original microporous structure. The pore size distribution of Fig. S4 shows that the pore size of ZIF-8 and Ag-Phy $@$ ZIF-8 are less than $2\ \mathrm{nm}$ and the comparative area data are shown in Table 1, which indicated the Ag and Phy were successfully encapsulated and did not change the structure of ZIF-8, maintaining the original good microporous structure.
Thereafter, we synthesized $\mathrm{Ag-Phy}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{HA}$ nanomaterials by surface modification with HA. As shown in Fig. 2d, the surface Zeta potential of the nanomaterials changed from $+19.67\pm2.55\mathrm{{\mV}}$ to $-21.07\pm1.19\mathrm{mV}$ , because HA comes with many carboxyl groups that present negative charges. Further, the quantitative analysis of AgPhy@ZIF-8@HA composites was performed, the drug loading capacity (DLC) and drug loading efficiency (DLE) of $\mathrm{Ag-Phy}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8@\mathrm{HA}$ were about $11.87\%$ and $89.63\%$ by analysing standard curve Fig. S5; The inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) analysis showed that $\mathrm{Ag-Phy}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{HA}$ contained $6.65~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Ag. Fig. S6 The DLS results showed that the particle size of Ag-Phy@ZIF-8@HA was about $200~\mathrm{{nm}}$ .
## 2.2. Stability assay of Ag-Phy@ZIF-8@HA
We investigated the stability of Ag-Ph $\operatorname{y}@\operatorname{ZIF-8}\ @\operatorname{HA}$ . First, ther­ mogravimetric analysis (TG) was performed, as shown in Fig. 3a the mass loss at 100 to $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was related to the evaporation of water molecules and the decomposition of hyaluronic acid, further weight loss was achieved at about $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ due to the carbonization of ZIF-8 organic ligands， and the loss above $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was mainly due to the collapse of the material skeleton. After that, the physiological stability was studied, and the $\mathrm{Ag-Phy}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{HA}$ composite nanomaterials were immersed in deionized water, PBS $\mathrm{(pH}7.4)$ , and $10\%$ fetal bovine serum (FBS) so­ lution for 3 days. As shown XRD in Fig. 3b and SEM in Fig. 3c, the characteristic peaks and morphology of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{ig-Phy}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{HA}$ did not change significantly. And as the analysis of the supernatant Ag con­ centration measured using ICP-MS in Fig. S7 showed, there was no obvious $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ leakage after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ immersion, the DLS in Fig. S8 indicated that the size of Ag-Phy $@$ ZIF-8 also did not change significantly. It was jointly demonstrated that Ag-Phy $@$ ZIF-8@HA nanocomposites have not only good thermal stability but also good physiological stability.
## 2.3. In vitro drug release study of Ag-Phy@ZIF-8@HA
Next, the release behavior of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ and Phy from Ag-Phy@ZIF-8@HA were investigated in different solutions of PBS $\mathrm{(pH~}5.5$ , 6.8 and 7.4). The release of Phy drug was measured with a UV spectrophotometer at $433~\mathrm{nm}$ , while the release behavior of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ was measured by ICP-MS. In Fig. $_{4\mathrm{a}}$ , it can be observed that after $120\mathrm{h}$ , the cumulative release of Phy in $\mathrm{PBS}\ \mathrm{pH}\ 5.5$ and $\mathtt{p H}6.8$ were $74.97\%$ and $67.40\%$ , respectively. The reason why was that the breakage of the coordination bond between $\scriptstyle2\ n^{2+}$ and imidazole led to the structural of ZIF-8 was disintegrated to release Phy in acidic conditions. However, at $\mathrm{pH}~7.4$ , physiological environment, the release of Phy was very slow, whose cumulative release was only $29.90\%$ after $120\mathrm{h}$ . The results showed that the release of Phy in acidic conditions was about 2.5 times higher than that in physiological conditions, which can be attributed to the fact that Ag$\operatorname{Phy}@\operatorname{ZIF-}8@\operatorname{HA}$ nanocomposites are more stable in the physiological environment. Thus Phy is released in a controlled manner.
Considering the sudden release of silver, excessive release of silver and accumulation of silver in the body, which can cause harm to the organism. We investigated the $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ release from $\mathtt{A g\mathrm{-Phy}@Z I F{-}8@H A}$ nanocomposites. As shown in Fig. 4b, the total content of ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ was $31.93\pm0.35~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ , and the release of ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ was monitored in PBS (pH 5.5 and pH 7.4) for 5 consecutive days. At $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ , the release of ${\mathbf{A}}{\mathbf{g}}^{+}$ has been very flat, and the ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ release was only $0.31\pm0.019\upmu\mathrm{g/L}$ after 5 days. However, at $\mathrm{pH}5.5$ , the ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ release reached $1.00\pm0.01\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ , which is nearly 3 times higher than that at $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ . This may be attrib­ uted to the pH-sensitive property of $\mathtt{A g-P h y}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{HA}$ , which was disintegrated under acidic conditions, resulting in Ag NPs can be released from it. Ag NPs are oxidized to ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ in the presence of oxygen and hydrogen, which can be represented by $4\mathrm{Ag\NPs+O_{2(aq)}+4H^{+}=}$ $4\mathrm{Ag}^{+}(\mathrm{aq})+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ [48]. Thus, the release of ${\mathbf{A}}{\mathbf{g}}^{+}$ increases under acidic conditions. ˙In a word, this pH-sensitive $\mathtt{A g-P h y@Z I F-8@H A}$ composite nanomaterial facilitates bacterial elimination due to the combined release of Phy and ${\mathbf{A}}{\mathbf{g}}^{+}$ [49].
## 2.4. Study of antibacterial properties of Ag-Phy@ZIF-8@HA nanomaterials
To investigate the interaction between Ag and Phy, we evaluated the inhibition circle size of four different nanocomposites (ZIF- $8@\mathrm{HA},$ ${\tt A g@Z I F-8@H A}$ , Phy@ZIF-8@HA, $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ -Phy@ZIF- $8@\mathrm{HA})$ against $E_{\sun}$ . coli $(\mathbf{G}^{-})$ and $s.$ . aureus $(\mathbf{G}^{+})$ . As we can see from Fig. 4c and Table S1, the largest inhibition circles around $\mathrm{Ag-Phy}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{HA}$ nanocomposites was about $20~\mathrm{{nm}}$ for $E.$ . coli and S. aureus, while the positive control (streptomycin) had an inhibition circle size of only about $23\ \mathrm{nm}$ .
Although inhibition circles were also observed around other composites, they were not as large as those of Ag-Phy $@$ ZIF -8@HA nanocomposites, which qualitatively demonstrates the synergistic antibacterial effect between $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ and Phy. Based on this exciting result, we quantitatively investigated the synergistic antibacterial ability of Ag and Phy and evaluated the minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC) of Ag-Phy@ZIF$8@\mathrm{HA}$ nanocomposites against E. coli and S. aureus using a double test tube dilution broth method. Fig. 4d and Fig. S9 demonstrate the MIC values of ${\tt A g@Z I F-8@H A}$ , $\operatorname{Phy}@\operatorname{ZIF}\cdot8@\operatorname{HA}$ , and $\mathrm{Ag-Phy}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{HA}$ nanocomposites, which indicate their ability to inhibit the growth of the strains. The measured data showed that the MIC values of Ag$\operatorname{Phy}@\operatorname{ZIF-}8@\operatorname{HA}$ against $E$ . coli and S. aureus were $7.98~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ Ag, $1.43\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ Phy, $3.99\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ Ag, $0.72~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ Phy, respectively. Futher, the MIC values for ${\tt A g@Z I F-8@H A}$ and $\operatorname{Phy}@\operatorname{ZIF-}8@\operatorname{HA}$ against $E.$ coli and S. aureus were $43.30~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ Ag, $9.57~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ Phy, $21.65~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ Ag, $2.40~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ Phy, respectively. We can see that when Ag-Phy $@Z\mathrm{IF}$ - ${8@\mathrm{HA}}$ nanocomposites acted on E. coli and S. aureus, the amount of inhibition by $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ and Phy were greatly reduced compared to the amount of inhibition of strains using ${\sf A g@Z I F-8@H A}$ and $\operatorname{Phy}@\operatorname{ZIF-}8@\operatorname{HA}$ nanocomposites, which may be due to the synergistic effect between Ag and Phy. To assess the synergistic effect, the fractional inhibition concentration (FIC) index was calculated [50]. The FIC index values for E. coli and S. aureus were 0.333 and 0.484, respectively, which were less than 0.5, and the results indicated that there was a synergistic effect between Ag and Phy.
More importantly, to further investigate the remarkable antibacterial properties of Ag-Phy@ZIF-8@HA nanomaterials, we measured the growth curves of E. coli and S. aureus when interacting with different nanocomposites. The viability of both bacteria was expressed by measuring the absorbance at $600\mathrm{nm}$ during a $24\mathrm{h}$ incubation time. As shown in Fig. 5a and b, Ag-Phy@ZIF- ${\&}{\tt@}{\mathrm{\textperthousand}}$ has the strongest inhibition effect at MIC, and the bactericidal rate could reach more than $90\%$ after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ of action, but at half MIC concentration of $\mathrm{Ag-Phy}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{HA}_{\mathrm{:}}$ , there was a relatively strong inhibition effect only in the first $^\textrm{\scriptsize8h}$ $\operatorname{Phy}@\operatorname{ZIF-}8@\operatorname{HA}$ and ${\tt A g@Z I F-8@H A}$ treatment groups had poor inhi­ bition, and the growth of strains was observed after $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ of treatment, while the minimal antibacterial effect of ZIF- ${\&}{\tt G R A}$ ruled out interfer­ ence by itself. The results suggest that the inhibition of Ag and Phy in Ag$\operatorname{Phy}@\operatorname{ZIF-}8@\operatorname{HA}$ may be enhanced by the presence of other components, and that there is an extremely strong inhibition of both typical strains. This may be attributed to two reasons, on the one hand, the strain metabolism produces hyaluronidase (HAase), which leads to enrich Ag$\operatorname{Phy}@\operatorname{ZIF-}8@\operatorname{HA}$ nanomaterials around the strain and breakdown hy­ aluronic acid (HA), on the other hand, the structural of ZIF-8 was dis­ integrated in the acidic microenvironment around the strain, which enhances the release of Ag and Phy.
In order to better observe the antibacterial effect of Ag-Phy@ZIF$8@\mathrm{HA}$ , plate counting method was taken and both strains were coincubated with PBS as control, with ZIF- ${\&}{\ @\mathrm{{HA}}}$ , ${\bf A}{\bf g}@{\bf Z}{\bf I}{\bf F}{\boldsymbol{-}}8@{\bf H}{\bf A}.$ , $\operatorname{Phy}@\operatorname{ZIF-}8@\operatorname{HA}$ and $\mathrm{Ag-Phy}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{HA}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Afterwards, $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the mixture was aspirated onto LB agar dishes and incubated overnight in an incubator at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The bacterial kill rate was obtained by counting the colonies on the dishes. The group of strains treated with ZIF- ${\&}{\tt@}{\mathrm{\textperthousand}}$ showed negligible bacterial inhibition compared to the control group, while over $99\%$ of the bacteria were killed when exposed to MIC of AgPhy@ZIF-8@HA Fig. 5c and Fig. S10. Finally, the morphology of the two bacterias treated with different nanocomposites were observed by SEM as shown in Fig. 5d, and we could clearly observe that the untreated E. coli and S. aureus showed an intact cell structure, full of intracellular material, and normal morphology. Bacterial morphology was different for each bacterial strain after being treated with various nanomaterials. The two strains treated with ZIF- ${\&}{\widetilde{\varrho}}_{\mathrm{HA}}$ showed little change in cell morphology, while a few contents were leaked from bacteria, being treated with $\operatorname{Phy}@\operatorname{ZIF-}8@\operatorname{HA}$ and ${\tt A g@Z I F-8@H A}$ . After the Ag$\operatorname{Phy}@\operatorname{ZIF-}8@\operatorname{HA}$ nanocomposite treated group compared with the con­ trol group, the appearance of the two strains were significantly changed, the cell structure was collapsed, the leakage of intracellular material was obvious, and the cell membrane had been completely disintegrated. This may be because Phy possess hydroxyl groups at C1 and C8 which are active against the micro-organisms to alter the permeability of the cell membrane to inhibit bacterial growth. Moreover, the incompleteness of the cell membrane increased the chance of Ag penetration into bacteria, which leads to leakage the contents of bacteria [51]. Fig. S11 shows the adhesion of E. coli and S. aureus on the $\mathtt{A g-P h y}@\mathtt{Z I F-}8@\mathtt{H A}$ material, from which can be seen that there is no S. aureus on the surface of the material and there are very few $E$ . coli fragments (red arrows) on the surface of the material. Since the acidic environment around bacteria makes the material release cubic Ag NPs (yellow box), this result indi­ cated that $\mathrm{Ag-Phy}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{HA}$ has excellent antibacterial activity and bacterial anti-adhesive properties.
## 2.5. Compatibility assay
In previous reports, it was proposed that ${\bf A g\ N P s}$ and ${\mathbf{A}}{\mathbf{g}}^{+}$ exhibit cytotoxicity to normal cells because ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ can permeate cell membranes and cause impairment of normal cellular metabolism. Therefore, it is necessary to do a detailed cytotoxicity test on the synthesized biomaterials. In this study, 24 h MTT was performed using RAW 264.7 cells to analyze the cytotoxicity of the synthesized samples. The results are shown in Fig. 6a. Since HA is a natural polysaccharide, the value of $\mathbf{A}_{490}$ was added compared with control group and showed no toxicity within $24\mathrm{h}$ . Due to the surface coating of HA, the cytotoxicity is reduced and the cell viability is above $80\%$ , when the concentration of ZIF$8@\mathrm{HA}$ , ${\mathrm{Ag}}@{\mathrm{ZIF}}{\cdot}8@{\mathrm{HA}}.$ , $\operatorname{Phy}@\operatorname{ZIF-}8@\operatorname{HA}$ and Ag-Phy $@$ ZIF-8 are less than $62.5~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ , which have minimal toxic effects on cells. Although the cytotoxicity of A $\operatorname{g-Phy}@\operatorname{ZIF-}8@\operatorname{HA}$ at $250~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ concentration was slightly higher than the other experimental groups, its cell viability was still higher than $75\%$ , indicating that this composite is capable of practical antimicrobial applications.
Further, the hemocompatibility of the biocomposites was also eval­ uated using red blood cells (RBCs) hemolysis assay. After treatment of RBCs with different concentrations of Ag- $\operatorname{shy}@\operatorname{ZIF-}8@\operatorname{HA}$ bio­ composites, unhemolysed RBCs were precipitated to the bottom by low speed and $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of supernatant was aspirated into a 96-well plate, and finally the value was measured at $576\ \mathrm{nm}\ (\mathrm{A}_{576})$ using a microplate reader. The RBCs treated with PBS and water were used as negative and positive controls, respectively. As shown in Fig. 6b and c, the hemolysis rate of RBCs treated with different concentrations of Ag-Phy@ZIF$8@\mathrm{HA}$ composite was small, and no significant hemolysis was observed when the concentration was as high as $150~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ that was already higher than the MIC of S. aureus and the hemolysis rate was less than $5\%$ . The results confirmed that this composite had good hemocompatibility.
## 2.6. Antibacterial mechanism
In the present study, the synergistic antibacterial mechanism of Ag$\operatorname{Phy}@\operatorname{ZIF-}8@\operatorname{HA}$ nanomaterials may be explained as follows Fig. 7. (I) Bacterial growth secretes hyaluronidase (HAase), which breaks down HA on the nanomaterial, causing the nanomaterial to enrich around the bacteria [52]. (II) Surrounding environment of bacteria is slightly acidified when bacteria metabolize, which allows specific cleavage of pH-responsive nanomaterials and release of Phy and ${\bf A g}\ {\bf N P s}$ [53]. (III) The released Ag NPs can adhere to the cell membrane surface and oxidize the surface proteins on the membrane to release ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ , resulting in increased cell membrane permeability and disruption of cell morphology. (IV) The increase of cell membrane permeability makes Phy enter more into the cell, which can affect the respiratory meta­ bolism of bacteria [54,55], and inhibit protein synthesis to kill bacteria. Therefore, the combination of the two exerts a synergistic antibacterial effect.
## 3. Conclusion
In this study, we successfully developed a novel, pH-responsive antimicrobial composite nanomaterial $\mathrm{Ag-Phy}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{HA}$ , which is designed to overcome the problem of bacterial resistance and exert a synergistic antimicrobial effect. Due to the efficient synergistic effect of silver and Phy, the $\mathrm{Ag-Phy}@\mathrm{ZIF-}8@\mathrm{HA}$ nanocomposite showed extremely strong antibacterial activity and resistance to bacterial adhesion, significantly inhibiting the growth and reproduction of bac­ teria $(99.1\%$ for E. coli and $99.5\%$ for S. aureus). In addition, this ma­ terial showes good biocompatibility and exhibited low toxicity to RAW 264.7 cells, probably due to the controlled release of drugs and surfacecoated HA. Thus, this work provides a new idea for the research of smart dressings for wounds and antimicrobial medical materials.
